Watch EndpointSlices. As of Kubernetes 1.33 the v1 Endpoints API is officially deprecated, and the API server returns warnings to anyone who reads or writes Endpoints. New Service features such as dual-stack networking and traffic distribution are supported only through EndpointSlices. Service proxies, Gateway API implementations and similar controllers have all been ported to EndpointSlices, and kube-proxy no longer uses Endpoints at all. The plan is also for conformance to stop requiring the Endpoints controller. Clusters may then run without Endpoints objects being generated, so a controller that depends on them could stop working.

When you consume EndpointSlices, keep in mind that a Service can have several of them, not exactly one as with Endpoints. Select them with the label `kubernetes.io/service-name=<service>`. You will typically see multiple slices in three cases:
- dual-stack Services get separate IPv4 and IPv6 slices;
- endpoints targeting different ports go in separate slices, for example during a port-change rollout;
- Services with more than 100 endpoints are split across slices.

Your controller has to combine all the slices for a Service to get its full backend set.
